From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: misc-de <misc-de@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:15:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ofono_push_notification, routectld: fetch from the carrier's
 MMSC only

A notification is an SMS anyone can send. mmsd looked up and fetched
whatever host its Content-Location named, and aiohttp followed up to
ten redirects whose targets nobody checked: a server of the sender
could lead each fetch on to 127.0.0.1 or into the home network, up to
21 requests per SMS with the retries. The address filter let private
networks and the device's own addresses through, and routectld routed
such an address (the home router, say) onto the MMS bearer for as
long as the fetch lived. The DNS query alone told the sender that the
phone is on, even without mobile data.

Now:
- Only the MMSC of the MMS context is fetched from: the Content-Location
  host must be the MMSC's host or one of its domain. Any other host is
  neither looked up nor fetched. Without an MMSC (no MMS from the
  carrier) nothing is fetched automatically.
- Redirects are never followed, on fetching, confirming and sending. A
  redirect of the MMSC is final, without retries.
- Only addresses on the internet are fetched from; private ones only
  for the MMSC's own host, as some carriers keep it in the MMS bearer's
  network.
- routectld sets no host route for an address of this device, of a
  network on one of its other links (WLAN, USB, hotspot) or of a network
  routed over another interface (a VPN). The cellular bearers and their
  CLATs are left out of this, they share the carrier's networks.
---
 mmsd/ofono_mms_service.py       |  6 ++-
 mmsd/ofono_push_notification.py | 77 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 routectld/routectld.py          | 43 ++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 115 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

